Subject: Jurassic Park (Electric fences) 

Tim May takes issue with my claim that the boy should not have been
shocked.

> If this is always so, then people could easily get over electrified fences
> by simply leaping onto them (very carefully), by placing wooden or
> insulating steps near the fence, or by wearing rubber-soled shoes, and then
> by leaping off on the other side. (I don't believe such is the case.)

I thought of this too. I assumed the fences contained lines with
different voltages.

> I confess to not knowing the exact way electrified fences work, but I don't
> think they can be circumvented just by preventing a good ground. 

I don't know either. I suppose it could be that a charge builds up on
anything that touches the line, so that there is a transient current
net flow into the offending object. Or that the object acts as a
transmission line through which a voltage pulse travels. This would
explain the eagles, at least.

If anybody knows for sure, don't be shy. (Everyone here is so reticent
about sharing their opinions.)

> It may be that different wires of the fence carry different voltages, or
> a.c. out of phase, in which case the Jurassic Park kid was standing on one
> wire and holding another wire with his hands...plausible then, for all
> these reasons, that he'd be zapped.

Except that there were wires between all the lines, which would have
shorted if the lines were at different voltages. Without all those
wires, they could have just crawled between the main lines, instead of
climbing over the whole thing.

