Date: Mon, 28 Jun 93 9:57:37 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: The Game of "Go" as an Example

Yikes!  Busy weekend for the seminar!

This talk of Go, evolution, and complexity is very interesting.
I'd like to try to add a couple of thoughts.

First, the mention of opponent strategy is quite important.  In
chess, a widely-believed conjecture (not universally believed,
but widely) is that were the entire game tree to be searched with
minimax, it would turn out that the game is a draw under optimal
strategy.  If this is true, then how are we to define the fitness
"landscape" of chess programs?  One way would be to compare its
performance to that of (any) optimal strategy.  But to me this
seems unreasonable because the program will never be asked to play
against the optimal strategy.  A better measure is probably how well
it does against its opponents -- human beings and machines with
limited computational resources.  In this case, good strategy depends
on the strategy of opponents and is a collection of empirically-discovered
strategy and tactical "rules" (Tim's shelf of Go books).  Go is a
better example of this than chess, because chess machines have overtaken
all but the best handful of humans by being brute force tree searchers.
This approach hasn't (yet) proven best for Go.

The Options for playing good chess/Go:
  * short program, long running time
  * long program, shorter running time

On Tim's program size/running time graph, the set of "good" go players
form a curve that asymptotes to each axis in the limit -- simple tree
search given humongous computing resources, and simple table lookup
given humongous program size.

The important points about this:
  * All "complex" "functionalities" will exhibit this property
  * Physical complexity or program complexity are required for high
    performance given computational constraints.  Since the physical
    world has such constraints, it's not surprising to see evolutionary
    processes generate complex physical structure, chasing the low-compute-
    time branch of the curve.
  * The structure of the curve in the tradeoff region is quite important.
  * Evolution is limited in what parts of the space it will search.
    It may be that a nanotech frog will be a superior sort of frog
    and have less "complexity" -- but natural processes of evolution
    (biological or perhaps even industrial) won't find it.
  * The nature of that curve is exactly what Bennett begins to
    characterize with his Logical Depth definition, though the fit
    is only partial.  Consider the logical depth of a "good" strategy
    for playing Go or doing what a Frog does (including its "trivial"
    reproduction skills).  Our "strings" are some encoding of the
    good strategies.  If we were to graph Bennett's logical depth as
    running time vs his parameter 's' (roughly, program size) of all
    the elements of the set, we should get a graph similar to the one
    described above.

A further note on complexity, implied by the above.  I'm starting
to believe that maybe the "classical" AIT view of complexity may
be the appropriate one for living things after all.  In the past
I've argued that the incompressibility version of complexity doesn't
work because it would imply a the post-blender frog is more complex
that the pre-blender frog -- in conflict with intuition.  However,
if we can somehow attach the stipulation that the object under study
exists on a complex fitness landscape (synonymous with the above
"complex" "functionality") with high fitness, the post-blender frog
has high complexity as fertilizer, but isn't much of a frog!

derek
then again, neither am I...


