Date: Mon, 28 Jun 93 10:23:59 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: "Information"

Tim May:

> I share Hal's hopes--and those of Nick Szabo and Derek Zahn, I think--that
> this stuff may give us a handle on the complexity of life and
> self-reproducing systems.

Yes.  In this vein, I'd like to refine a bit the problems involved in
deciding how big the "program" for my pre-blender frog would actually
be.  That is, despite its having more structure than the post-blender
frog, it's still made up of lots of "random" pieces at the micro-scale.

There are two takes on this that I can think of.  First, we can
look at phase space volumes.  That is, instead of the exact positions
of individual particles being requred for a "specification", we
could think of volumes of phase space that are "equivalent" to
the current configuration in terms of the position of the frog on
the frog's fitness landscape and reduce the complexity by factoring
out such equivalence.

Alternatively, we can use the frog's genome, but that route seems
to be fraught with difficulty -- but very interesting difficulty!
The problem is that physics isn't a turing machine -- it's not
even close.  The clean notion of logical depth depends to a large
extent on the finite (simple!) control of the machine.  I'd like
to define the parameters of a general "Darwin Machine" that would
be laid out with the analytical clarity of the TM; this would allow
us to pin down the idea of evolutionary computation and tie it
formally to Logical Depth.  Unfortunately, a DM has a fantastically
complex control structure which makes it much more difficult to
treat with the same analytical style as the TM.  Frown.

derek

