From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: The Game of "Go" as an Example

James A. Donald, replying to Hal Finney:

> > Intuitively it seems unlikely that the GA would fail to produce a good
> > Go program.  If a very good Go player could in fact be produced in this
> > way, it would challenge the applicability of the AIT measure of complexity
> > to evolutionary processes.
> 
> The method you describe of evolving programs - start of from random stuff 
> and then throw them in at the deep end - will always evolve them 
> to a local optimum.  I would expect that in the case of a hard problem 
> like go, the local optimum is trivial.

I largely agree with this, and think that it is an issue of tremendous
importance for the development of Genetic Programming beyond its
current state.  Despite all the enthusiasm for it, GP still hasn't
solved more than the usual toy AI problems.

Here's what I think the difficulty is:  the genetic algorithm works
for the following reason:  through the process of fitness-proportional
reproduction, gene combinations called "schemata" are reproduced
in such a way that the better schemata get exponentially-increasing
representation in the population of individuals.  This happens without
the unreasonably burdensome explicit bookkeeping of schemata fitness.
John Holland proved this.  He also proved that this is exactly what
we'd want to do to maximize the expected return, given that the
schemata are independent.

This independence assumption is referred to as the "Building Block
Hypothesis" (BBH).  When it holds, GA is a very efficient at climbing to
really good solutions.  When it doesn't, it bombs.  Problems where
the Building Block Hypothesis doesn't hold are called "deceptive".

It seems to me that the "schemata" of genetic programming flagrantly
violate the BBH.  The relationship between program fragments is much
more complex than an independence assumption can be comfortable with.
Look at the problems to which GP has been applied -- symbolic
regression, truck backing-up, etc.  Most of these have small solutions
that are constructed as combinations of partially-successful complete
solutions.  That's not the way to build a complex program.

Possible solutions:

 1) (as James implies): Guided evolution, through intermediate stages
    of increasing difficulty.  Our explorations into complexity may
    shed some light on how to do this, by giving us a handle on
    problem difficulty.  We evolved in a similar way, though there
    was no guiding hand as far as we know -- just a naturally increase
    in the complexity of fitness landscapes caused by environmental
    diversity.

 2) Despite the independence problems, there still is a (weak)
    correlation between useful schemata and the fitness of the
    organisms that incorporate them.  If we merely increase the
    size of the population and run it longer, these correlations
    should win out eventually.  The key questions are how big and
    how fast.
  
 3) We can redefine the schemata, either by changing the semantics
    of the "language" being interpreted, or changing the genetic
    operators and reproduction mechanisms that alter schema
    concentrations in the population.
  
 4) We can change the fitness functions to encourage interesting
    behavior even if the organism itself doesn't perform well.
    This could most effictively be done (IMO) with multidimensional
    fitness measures.  Hill climbing has no real difficulty if the
    fitness surface is multi-dimensional.

