Date: Mon, 28 Jun 93 12:37:23 EDT
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: NANO:Critiques of Drexler

James A. Donald <jamesdon@infoserv.com> writes some criticisms of
Drexler's nanotech ideas, from a perspective that seems tilted toward
biological molecules floating in solution:

> The fundamental problem in constructing nanostructures is that it is 
> extremely difficult to predict how the nano structure will behave, 
> for example the unsolved, and perhaps insoluble, protein folding problem.
> 
> Drexler is aware of this problem and rightly proposes to deal with this 
> by creating nanostructures out of components whose consequences and 
> effects are extremely isolated...

While the second paragraph is correct, the first gives the impression that
the folding behavior of large free-floating proteins has something ("the 
fundamental problem") to do with nanotech, which it doesn't much.

> Unfortunately the resulting designs are violently chemically unstable.  
> A random molecule banging about inside them will have the same effect 
> as monkey wrench in your car gearbox, causing everything to randomly 
> rearrange in a violently exothermic fashion.

This is "molecules in solution" thinking.  You don't design a machine
so that random monkeywrenches can get into it.  What you do is put a
box around it.  Also, can someone give a second opinion about the 
designs being unstable in the sense of heavy use of bonds and
arrangements with high potential energy?
 
> Drexler ignores the problem of friction.  Friction becomes more and more 
> severe as structures with moving parts get smaller and smaller.  The 
> only way to deal with this problem is the way that cells deal with it.  

I have looked at Drexler's designs for bearings.  I don't know what he
does with sliding friction, but nanomachines are in *vacuum* (i.e., what
you get when you make a box with nothing in it), not solution.  In my
experience with mechanical design, one designs enough space around the 
moving parts that they aren't bumping into things.  Also I assume there 
are rack-and-pinion designs for really big sliders.

Mr. Donald suggests molecules in solution as a way to reduce friction.
He points out that this is difficult to design, but I think the main
problem is that it's difficult to mix the two styles of molecules-
-moving-randomly-in-solution with hard-deterministic-machines-in-
vacuum-boxes.

-fnerd
